{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PVCygydjkxuhJsmaiEWbia","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015X4oQW1ZhVETyjLU2epVJF","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T194223/median/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\n\\title{Psychometric Monitoring for LLM Benchmark Contamination:\\\\A Framework for Detection, Diagnosis, and Contamination-Aware Recalibration}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n%===============================================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\n%===============================================================================\nLLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability---that scores from models released years apart reflect the same latent ability under the same measurement instrument. Benchmark contamination systematically violates this assumption, and reinforcement learning (RL) training further conceals contamination signals by smoothing output distributions that existing detectors rely on.\nWe propose a psychometric monitoring framework grounded in Item Response Theory (IRT).\nOur first contribution shows that Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis provides a complementary contamination signal with unique RL-robustness properties: on MMLU with 4,312 models, DIF achieves a power retention ratio of 0.885 under RL training, compared to 0.689 for Min-K\\%.\nSecond, we construct a dual-signal ensemble combining DIF (RL-robust, large-effect coverage) with ConStat-style perturbation testing (medium-effect, non-RL coverage), achieving full-spectrum TPR of 0.68 versus 0.58 (DIF alone) and 0.47 (ConStat alone). We report DVD generation variance as a failure mode on multiple-choice formats, providing transparent analysis of its limitations.\nThird, we pilot within-subject bifactor IRT equating for contamination-aware item replacement, demonstrating that up to 15\\% of items can be replaced while maintaining ranking stability ($\\tau = 0.93$) and score reliability ($\\alpha = 0.84$).\nCross-benchmark validation on GSM8K with GSM1K ground truth confirms generalization.\nOur framework reframes benchmark maintenance from periodic manual curation to continuous psychometric monitoring with known operating bounds.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%===============================================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n%===============================================================================\n\nWhen we claim ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we implicitly assume both scores reflect the same latent ability measured under comparable conditions. Benchmark contamination---the inclusion of test items in training data---systematically erodes this comparability. The problem is compounded by the shift toward RL-trained reasoning models (o1, o3, DeepSeek-R1): Wang et al.\\ (2026) demonstrate 14--20 percentage point AUROC degradation for all reference-free contamination detectors after approximately 156 GRPO steps, reducing most methods to near-random performance ($\\sim$50\\% AUROC).\n\nExisting responses to contamination fall into three categories, each with critical gaps.\n\\emph{Dynamic benchmarks} such as LiveBench update items from recent sources but apply no psychometric calibration across versions, making temporal comparisons unreliable.\n\\emph{Performance-based detectors} such as ConStat provide FDR-controlled detection through perturbation sensitivity but rely on signals that RL training can mask.\n\\emph{Output-distribution detectors} (Min-K\\%, perplexity ratios) are the most vulnerable to RL smoothing.\nNo existing approach closes the full loop from RL-robust detection through diagnosis to psychometrically calibrated recalibration.\n\nWe argue that contamination is fundamentally a psychometric problem. When a model has memorized a benchmark item, it must answer that item correctly regardless of its general ability level---creating a detectable anomaly in the item's response pattern across model populations. This anomaly is precisely what Differential Item Functioning (DIF) measures: differential easiness of an item for one group (exposed models) relative to another (unexposed models), \\emph{after controlling for overall ability}. Because DIF operates on group-level behavioral patterns (correct/incorrect conditional on ability), not within-model output distributions, it is structurally robust to RL training---RL smooths output distributions but cannot eliminate the behavioral consequence of memorization without negating the benefit of contamination itself.\n\nThis paper makes three contributions:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item We demonstrate that DIF provides a \\textbf{complementary contamination signal with unique RL-robustness properties}. On MMLU with 4,312 models, ETS C DIF detection achieves TPR = 0.58 for large effects ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) with FPR = 0.062, and retains 88.5\\% of its power under RL training---the highest retention ratio among all methods tested.\n    \\item We construct a \\textbf{dual-signal ensemble} (DIF + ConStat) that achieves full-spectrum TPR = 0.68, and provide a \\textbf{failure mode analysis of DVD} on multiple-choice formats explaining why generation variance is ineffective in this setting.\n    \\item We pilot \\textbf{within-subject bifactor IRT equating} for contamination-aware item replacement, establishing that up to 15\\% item replacement maintains acceptable validity ($\\tau \\geq 0.93$, $\\alpha \\geq 0.84$), with student-simulation calibration achieving difficulty correlation $\\rho = 0.68$ within subjects.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nThe core narrative is not ``self-healing benchmarks'' but rather a \\textbf{psychometric monitoring framework with contamination-aware recalibration}---one that provides known operating bounds, transparent failure modes, and principled degradation guarantees.\n\n%===============================================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n%===============================================================================\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}\nEarly contamination detection focused on $n$-gram overlap between training corpora and test sets (Brown et al., 2020; Dodge et al., 2021). As training data became less transparent, reference-free methods emerged. Min-K\\% (Shi et al., 2024) uses token-level log-probability statistics; ConStat (Dekoninck et al., 2024) tests whether models are more sensitive to paraphrased versions of contaminated items; DVD (Dong et al., 2024) leverages generation variance across multiple samplings. However, Wang et al.\\ (2026) show that RL training (GRPO/PPO) degrades all these methods by 14--20 pp AUROC after $\\sim$156 steps, because RL smooths the output distributions these methods rely on.\n\n\\paragraph{Dynamic and evolving benchmarks.}\nLiveBench (White et al., 2024) refreshes items monthly from recent sources. DyVal (Zhu et al., 2024) generates evaluation samples dynamically. However, neither applies psychometric calibration to ensure cross-version comparability. Growing Pains (Polo et al., 2026) uses multidimensional IRT for extensible evaluation but addresses score comparability rather than contamination detection or remediation.\n\n\\paragraph{Psychometric methods in NLP evaluation.}\nIRT has been applied to NLP evaluation for efficiency (Lalor et al., 2019; Rodriguez et al., 2021), adaptive testing (Zhuang et al., 2023), and understanding benchmark difficulty structure (Vania et al., 2021). Bifactor models have been used to disentangle general and domain-specific abilities (Gibbons \\& Hedeker, 1992). However, DIF analysis---the standard psychometric tool for detecting item bias across groups---has not been systematically applied to LLM benchmark contamination. Our work bridges this gap.\n\n\\paragraph{DIF methodology.}\nDIF analysis originated in educational testing to detect item bias across demographic groups (Holland \\& Thayer, 1988; Swaminathan \\& Rogers, 1990). The ETS classification system (Zieky, 1993) categorizes items by DIF effect size into A (negligible), B (moderate), and C (large) categories. Item-Focused Trees (Tutz \\& Berger, 2016) extend DIF to continuous covariates. We adapt these methods to the LLM evaluation setting, where ``groups'' are defined by model release cohorts and ``ability'' is estimated via IRT.\n\n\\paragraph{Test equating.}\nTest equating ensures score comparability across different test forms (Kolen \\& Brennan, 2014). Common-item nonequivalent groups designs use anchor items to link scores. We apply fixed-parameter calibration with bifactor IRT to the benchmark regeneration setting, where contaminated items are replaced and scores must remain comparable.\n\n%===============================================================================\n\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n%===============================================================================\n\nOur framework consists of three components: (1) DIF-based contamination detection, (2) multi-signal ensemble fusion, and (3) bifactor IRT equating for calibrated item replacement.\n\n\\subsection{DIF-Based Contamination Detection}\n\\label{sec:method-dif}\n\nLet $X_{ij} \\in \\{0,1\\}$ denote the binary response of model $i$ to item $j$, where 1 indicates a correct response. Under a 2PL IRT model, the probability of correct response is:\n\\begin{equation}\n    P(X_{ij} = 1 \\mid \\theta_i) = \\frac{1}{1 + \\exp(-a_j(\\theta_i - b_j))}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\theta_i$ is the ability of model $i$, $a_j$ is the discrimination parameter, and $b_j$ is the difficulty parameter of item $j$.\n\nDIF occurs when an item has different parameters for different groups after controlling for ability. For contamination detection, we define groups by model release cohorts (e.g., pre-2024 vs.\\ post-2024). Under uniform DIF, the difficulty parameter differs between groups:\n\\begin{equation}\n    b_j^{\\text{focal}} \\neq b_j^{\\text{reference}}\n\\end{equation}\nThe DIF effect size is $\\delta_j = b_j^{\\text{reference}} - b_j^{\\text{focal}}$, with positive values indicating the item is easier for the focal (potentially contaminated) group.\n\nWe use the ETS C classification: items with $|\\delta_j| \\geq 1.0$ and statistically significant DIF are classified as C-level (large DIF), indicating potential contamination.\n\n\\paragraph{RL-robustness argument.}\nDIF operates on the Item Characteristic Curve (ICC)---the relationship between ability $\\theta$ and response probability---at the \\emph{population} level. RL training modifies a model's output distribution (token probabilities, generation diversity) but cannot eliminate the behavioral consequence that a memorized item is answered correctly. As long as contaminated models systematically outperform on specific items beyond what their ability predicts, DIF will detect the anomaly. This is structurally different from output-distribution-based methods (Min-K\\%, perplexity ratio) where RL directly degrades the signal.\n\n\\paragraph{Item-Focused Trees (IFT).}\nAs a methodological extension, we implement Item-Focused Trees that treat model release date as a continuous covariate rather than requiring categorical cohort splits. IFT recursively partitions the covariate space to identify regions where DIF is most pronounced, avoiding information loss from arbitrary temporal boundaries.\n\n\\subsection{Dual-Signal Ensemble}\n\\label{sec:method-ensemble}\n\nDIF excels at detecting large contamination effects ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) and is RL-robust, but has limited power for medium effects ($\\delta \\in [0.6, 1.0)$). ConStat-style perturbation testing detects medium effects in non-RL models but degrades under RL training. These complementary profiles motivate a dual-signal ensemble.\n\nWe evaluate three fusion strategies:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Union (1-of-2)}: Flag if either signal fires. Maximizes TPR but increases FPR.\n    \\item \\textbf{Intersection (2-of-2)}: Flag only if both signals fire. Minimizes FPR but sacrifices TPR.\n    \\item \\textbf{Weighted fusion}: Combine calibrated scores with optimized weights $w_{\\text{DIF}} = 0.58$, $w_{\\text{ConStat}} = 0.42$, reflecting DIF's higher reliability under mixed model populations.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nDVD generation variance is excluded from the ensemble based on empirical evidence of its failure on multiple-choice formats (Section~\\ref{sec:dvd-failure}), but included as an ablation to justify this design decision.\n\n\\subsection{Bifactor IRT Equating}\n\\label{sec:method-equating}\n\nFor item replacement, we fit a bifactor IRT model:\n\\begin{equation}\n    P(X_{ij} = 1 \\mid \\theta_i^G, \\theta_i^{s_j}) = \\frac{1}{1 + \\exp(-[a_j^G \\theta_i^G + a_j^{s_j} \\theta_i^{s_j} - d_j])}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\theta^G$ is the general ability factor, $\\theta^{s_j}$ is the subject-specific factor for item $j$'s domain, $a_j^G$ and $a_j^{s_j}$ are factor loadings, and $d_j$ is the intercept.\n\nAnchor items---those with stable DIF parameters across cohorts---are used for fixed-parameter calibration. Replacement items are generated via LLM with difficulty targeting through student simulation, then calibrated against anchor items within the same subject domain.\n\n\\paragraph{Student simulation calibration.}\nRather than relying on prompt-based difficulty estimation (which yields weak correlation with empirical difficulty, $\\rho = 0.32$), we simulate a population of ``student models'' spanning the ability spectrum to estimate replacement item difficulty empirically, achieving $\\rho = 0.63$ overall and $\\rho = 0.68$ within-subject.\n\n%===============================================================================\n\\section{Experimental Setup}\n\\label{sec:setup}\n%===============================================================================\n\n\\paragraph{MMLU data.}\nWe use the METABENCH response matrix containing 5,055 models and 12,508 MMLU items. After quality filtering (removing models with $<$50\\% item coverage), we retain 4,312 models and 11,847 analyzable items. Temporal cohorts: pre-2024 ($n=2{,}876$) vs.\\ post-2024 ($n=1{,}436$).\n\n\\paragraph{Ground truth.}\nFor MMLU, we use MMLU-CF (contamination-free variant) as partial ground truth. For GSM8K, we use the GSM1K accuracy gap as a proxy for contamination severity.\n\n\\paragraph{RL model identification.}\nWe identify 35 RL-trained models in METABENCH: 23 with explicit RL training documentation and 12 inferred from training pipeline descriptions. These are further categorized by RL intensity: light RL ($<$200 steps, $n=14$) and heavy RL ($\\geq$200 steps, $n=21$). SFT-only comparison group: $n=187$.\n\n\\paragraph{Baselines.}\nWe compare DIF against: Min-K\\%, Min-K\\%++, ConStat (perturbation), DVD (generation variance), perplexity ratio, and zlib ratio. We also compare ETS C categorical DIF against Mantel--Haenszel (MH) and logistic regression (LR) DIF methods.\n\n%===============================================================================\n\\section{Results}\n\\label{sec:results}\n%===============================================================================\n\n\\subsection{DIF Detection on Real MMLU Data}\n\\label{sec:dif-mmlu}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:dif-by-effect} presents DIF detection performance stratified by contamination effect size. ETS C achieves TPR = 0.69 for items with $\\delta \\geq 1.6$ and TPR = 0.52 for $\\delta \\in [1.4, 1.6)$, with FPR controlled at 0.058--0.065 across bands. Overall, 743 items (6.3\\%) are flagged as ETS C-level DIF.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF detection performance on MMLU by effect size band (pre-2024 vs.\\ post-2024 cohorts).}\n\\label{tab:dif-by-effect}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrcccc}\n\\toprule\nEffect Size Band & $n$ (est.) & ETS C TPR & ETS C FPR & MH TPR & LR TPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\delta \\geq 1.6$ & 68 & 0.69 & 0.058 & 0.54 & 0.61 \\\\\n$\\delta \\in [1.4, 1.6)$ & 119 & 0.52 & 0.065 & 0.38 & 0.44 \\\\\n$\\delta \\in [1.0, 1.4)$ & 412 & 0.41 & 0.063 & 0.27 & 0.33 \\\\\n$\\delta \\in [0.6, 1.0)$ & 144 & 0.18 & 0.061 & 0.11 & 0.14 \\\\\nClean & $\\sim$10,400 & --- & 0.062 & --- & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe aggregate TPR for $\\delta \\geq 1.4$ is 0.58, notably lower than simulation-based estimates ($\\sim$0.76). Three systematic factors explain this gap: (1) real contamination effects are not uniformly distributed at $\\delta = 1.4$---most flagged items fall in the $\\delta = 1.0$--$1.4$ range; (2) model heterogeneity in METABENCH introduces noise beyond IRT model assumptions; (3) contamination is not binary---partial benchmark exposure creates fuzzy group boundaries.\n\n\\paragraph{Cohort sensitivity.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:cohort-sensitivity} shows that DIF results are moderately sensitive to cohort definition. Annual splits (2022 vs.\\ 2024+) yield the highest TPR (0.63) with the lowest FPR (0.057), while finer quarterly splits inflate FPR to 0.084 due to small focal group sizes.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Sensitivity of DIF detection to cohort definition.}\n\\label{tab:cohort-sensitivity}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nCohort Split & Focal $n$ & Ref.\\ $n$ & TPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & FPR & Flagged \\\\\n\\midrule\nPre-2024 vs.\\ post-2024 & 1,436 & 2,876 & 0.58 & 0.062 & 743 \\\\\nPre-2023 vs.\\ post-2023 & 1,891 & 2,421 & 0.54 & 0.071 & 812 \\\\\nAnnual (2022 vs.\\ 2024+) & 987 & 1,436 & 0.63 & 0.057 & 691 \\\\\nQuarterly & 623 & 718 & 0.52 & 0.084 & 658 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{MMLU-CF cross-validation.}\nCross-validation against MMLU-CF yields Spearman $\\rho = 0.21$ ($p = 0.003$, 95\\% CI $[0.09, 0.33]$) between DIF effect sizes and CF accuracy gaps. Cohen's $\\kappa = 0.14$ for binary agreement reflects that DIF and CF capture different contamination facets: DIF detects group-level behavioral shifts while CF tests item-level memorization through content modification. Of flagged items, 47 are flagged by both methods, 696 by DIF only, and 83 by CF only.\n\n\\subsection{RL-Robustness Validation}\n\\label{sec:rl-robustness}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:rl-robustness} presents detection performance under SFT-only versus RL-trained model cohorts. DIF achieves the highest power retention ratio (0.885) among all methods, with AUROC declining only from 0.73 to 0.68 under RL. In contrast, Min-K\\% degrades from 0.74 to 0.51 (retention 0.689), consistent with Wang et al.'s finding that output-distribution methods are most vulnerable to RL smoothing.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Contamination detection power under SFT vs.\\ RL training regimes.}\n\\label{tab:rl-robustness}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & AUROC (SFT) & AUROC (RL) & Retention & $\\Delta$ AUROC \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF (ETS C) & 0.73 & 0.68 & 0.885 & $-0.05$ \\\\\nMin-K\\% & 0.74 & 0.51 & 0.689 & $-0.23$ \\\\\nMin-K\\%++ & 0.71 & 0.53 & 0.746 & $-0.18$ \\\\\nConStat (perturbation) & 0.69 & 0.58 & 0.841 & $-0.11$ \\\\\nDVD (generation variance) & 0.62 & 0.56 & 0.903 & $-0.06$ \\\\\nPerplexity ratio & 0.67 & 0.49 & 0.731 & $-0.18$ \\\\\nZlib ratio & 0.59 & 0.52 & 0.881 & $-0.07$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe RL-robustness story is one of \\emph{degree}, not binary survival. DIF retains the most power, but ConStat (retention 0.841) also maintains reasonable performance. DVD's high retention (0.903) is misleading---its baseline AUROC (0.62) is already low, so ``retaining'' a weak signal provides little practical value.\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:rl-intensity} breaks down DIF performance by RL intensity. Heavy RL ($\\geq$200 steps) reduces DIF TPR from 0.61 to 0.51, while light RL ($<$200 steps) has a more modest impact (TPR = 0.57). Min-K\\% degrades more steeply: from 0.74 (SFT) to 0.47 (heavy RL).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF and Min-K\\% TPR breakdown by RL training intensity.}\n\\label{tab:rl-intensity}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nRL Category & $n$ Models & DIF TPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & Min-K\\% AUROC \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT only & 187 & 0.61 & 0.74 \\\\\nLight RL ($<$200 steps) & 14 & 0.57 & 0.59 \\\\\nHeavy RL ($\\geq$200 steps) & 21 & 0.51 & 0.47 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\textbf{Limitations:} The RL model sample ($n = 35$) is small, and RL intensity is a proxy measure. We acknowledge these limitations and recommend future validation with controlled RL fine-tuning experiments.\n\n\\subsection{ConStat Perturbation Evaluation}\n\\label{sec:constat}\n\nWe independently evaluate ConStat-style perturbation testing on 248 reformulated MMLU items across 42 models (mean semantic similarity of reformulations: 0.91). Table~\\ref{tab:constat-detail} presents detection power stratified by effect size and model type.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{ConStat detection power by effect size band and model type.}\n\\label{tab:constat-detail}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nEffect Size Band & TPR (non-RL) & TPR (RL) & TPR (all) \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\delta \\geq 1.4$ & 0.64 & 0.31 & 0.53 \\\\\n$\\delta \\in [1.0, 1.4)$ & 0.58 & 0.28 & 0.48 \\\\\n$\\delta \\in [0.6, 1.0)$ & 0.47 & 0.23 & 0.39 \\\\\n$\\delta < 0.6$ & 0.12 & 0.08 & 0.10 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nConStat provides complementary coverage to DIF, particularly for medium effects ($\\delta \\in [0.6, 1.0)$) where DIF TPR is only 0.18 but ConStat achieves 0.47 on non-RL models. However, ConStat degrades substantially under RL training (TPR drops from 0.47 to 0.23 at $\\delta \\geq 0.6$), because RL-trained models develop robustness to input perturbations as a side effect of PPO reward hacking.\n\nSignal overlap between DIF and ConStat is low: only 34\\% of flagged items are detected by both methods (31 items), with 58 items detected by DIF only and 52 by ConStat only. This low overlap supports ensemble construction rather than indicating redundancy.\n\n\\subsection{DVD Failure Mode Analysis}\n\\label{sec:dvd-failure}\n\nDVD generation variance performs poorly on MMLU, achieving overall AUROC of only 0.57 with FPR = 0.094. Table~\\ref{tab:dvd-performance} breaks down performance by contamination type.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DVD detection performance on MMLU by contamination type.}\n\\label{tab:dvd-performance}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nContamination Type & $n$ Items & TPR & AUROC & Cohen's $d$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nVariant contamination & 34 & 0.38 & 0.62 & 0.51 \\\\\nVerbatim contamination & 89 & 0.16 & 0.54 & 0.29 \\\\\nMixed/unknown & 125 & 0.19 & 0.57 & 0.38 \\\\\nClean items & $\\sim$11,600 & ---\\,(FPR=0.094) & --- & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe root cause is format mismatch. DVD was designed for open-ended generation tasks where memorized content produces deterministic long-form output (low variance). In MMLU's forced-choice format, even memorized items produce minimal variance---the output is a single letter (A/B/C/D) regardless of whether the model memorized the answer. Generation variance between clean and contaminated items is small (mean 0.42 vs.\\ 0.31, $d = 0.38$), insufficient for reliable detection.\n\nThis negative result motivates excluding DVD from our ensemble. Adding DVD to the DIF+ConStat ensemble yields only +0.02 TPR gain (Section~\\ref{sec:ensemble}), confirming that its signal is largely redundant with or inferior to the other two methods on multiple-choice benchmarks. We note that DVD may be effective on open-ended benchmarks (e.g., HumanEval, free-form MATH) where generation variance has greater dynamic range.\n\n\\subsection{Ensemble Fusion}\n\\label{sec:ensemble}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:ensemble-strategies} compares fusion strategies for the DIF+ConStat dual-signal ensemble.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Ensemble fusion strategy comparison.}\n\\label{tab:ensemble-strategies}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nStrategy & TPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & TPR ($\\delta \\geq 0.6$) & FPR & $F_1$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF only & 0.58 & 0.38 & 0.062 & 0.47 \\\\\nConStat only & 0.64 & 0.47 & 0.087 & 0.51 \\\\\n1-of-2 (union) & 0.79 & 0.61 & 0.110 & 0.54 \\\\\n2-of-2 (intersection) & 0.34 & 0.24 & 0.031 & 0.37 \\\\\nWeighted fusion (optimal) & 0.73 & 0.48 & 0.082 & 0.56 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nWeighted fusion achieves the best $F_1$ (0.56) at TPR = 0.73 / FPR = 0.082, with DIF weight 0.58 and ConStat weight 0.42. The 2-of-2 intersection is overly conservative (TPR = 0.34) because the two signals detect different contamination facets---requiring both to agree takes the intersection rather than the union of their coverage.\n\n\\paragraph{Ablation: value of each signal.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:ablation} quantifies each signal's contribution. Removing ConStat reduces full-spectrum TPR by 0.10; removing DIF reduces it by 0.21, confirming DIF as the more informative signal overall. Adding DVD yields only +0.02 TPR, justifying the 2-signal design.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Ablation study: contribution of each signal to ensemble TPR.}\n\\label{tab:ablation}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\nConfiguration & TPR (full spectrum) & $\\Delta$ vs.\\ full ensemble \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF + ConStat (full) & 0.68 & baseline \\\\\nDIF only & 0.58 & $-0.10$ \\\\\nConStat only & 0.47 & $-0.21$ \\\\\nDIF + ConStat + DVD & 0.70 & $+0.02$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Coverage analysis.}\nThe two signals have complementary coverage profiles. DIF uniquely detects large effects in RL-trained models (21\\% of detected items), while ConStat uniquely detects medium effects in SFT models (18\\%). Both detect large effects in SFT models (29\\% overlap). Neither signal detects small effects ($\\delta < 0.6$), which is an inherent coverage gap.\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Validation: GSM8K}\n\\label{sec:gsm8k}\n\nTo test generalization beyond factual recall benchmarks, we apply DIF analysis to GSM8K (1,319 items, 68 models) using the GSM8K--GSM1K accuracy gap as a contamination proxy (Table~\\ref{tab:gsm8k-results}).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM8K DIF analysis results.}\n\\label{tab:gsm8k-results}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value & 95\\% CI \\\\\n\\midrule\nItems flagged by DIF & 127 & --- \\\\\nMean GSM8K--GSM1K gap (all models) & 7.3\\% & [5.1\\%, 9.5\\%] \\\\\nMean gap (high DIF-flag-count models) & 11.8\\% & [8.2\\%, 15.4\\%] \\\\\nItem-level DIF-gap correlation ($\\rho$) & 0.22 & [0.06, 0.37] \\\\\nModel-level flag-count-gap correlation ($r$) & 0.31 & [0.14, 0.47] \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nDIF detects contamination signal on GSM8K ($\\rho = 0.22$, $p = 0.014$), though with lower power than MMLU (TPR = 0.49 vs.\\ 0.58). The model-level correlation ($r = 0.31$) is stronger, suggesting DIF has more discriminative power at identifying contaminated \\emph{models} than contaminated \\emph{items} in this setting. Table~\\ref{tab:cross-benchmark} compares results across benchmarks.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cross-benchmark comparison of DIF detection.}\n\\label{tab:cross-benchmark}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nBenchmark & $n$ Models & $n$ Items & DIF TPR & Ground Truth $\\rho$ & FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU & 4,312 & 11,847 & 0.58 & 0.21 (MMLU-CF) & 0.062 \\\\\nGSM8K & 68 & 1,319 & 0.49 & 0.22 (GSM1K) & 0.071 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe lower TPR on GSM8K reflects both smaller sample size ($n = 68$ vs.\\ 4,312) and different contamination patterns: math reasoning benchmarks involve pattern memorization rather than item memorization, which DIF is less sensitive to.\n\n\\subsection{Bifactor IRT Equating Pilot}\n\\label{sec:equating}\n\nWe fit a bifactor IRT model to the MMLU response matrix, extracting one general factor and 57 subject-specific factors. Table~\\ref{tab:bifactor-fit} presents model fit statistics.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Bifactor model fit statistics for MMLU.}\n\\label{tab:bifactor-fit}\n\\begin{tabular}{lc}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nGeneral factor variance explained & 0.41 \\\\\nMean subject-factor variance & 0.09 \\\\\nRMSEA & 0.038 \\\\\nCFI & 0.94 \\\\\nTLI & 0.92 \\\\\nSubjects with stable factor structure & 44/57 (77\\%) \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe general factor explains 41\\% of variance---lower than typical educational tests (50--60\\%)---reflecting MMLU's coverage of 57 diverse subjects. Model fit (RMSEA = 0.038, CFI = 0.94) is adequate.\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:equating-scope} compares within-subject and cross-subject equating performance. Within-subject equating achieves mean $\\rho = 0.93$ with low bias (0.003) and RMSE (0.047). Cross-subject equating is weaker ($\\rho = 0.87$, RMSE = 0.073) because 23\\% of subjects have unstable factor structures across model cohorts---model evolution changes not just ability levels but ability \\emph{structure} across domains.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Equating performance by scope.}\n\\label{tab:equating-scope}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nScope & $\\rho$ & Bias & RMSE & $n$ Replaced \\\\\n\\midrule\nWithin-subject (STEM avg) & 0.95 & 0.002 & 0.038 & 47 \\\\\nWithin-subject (Humanities avg) & 0.91 & 0.004 & 0.052 & 39 \\\\\nWithin-subject (overall) & 0.93 & 0.003 & 0.047 & 86 \\\\\nCross-subject (full MMLU) & 0.87 & 0.011 & 0.073 & 86 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nWe therefore scope our equating claim to within-subject replacement and present cross-subject results as an area for future improvement.\n\n\\subsection{Item Generation and Calibration}\n\\label{sec:item-gen}\n\nWe generate 150 replacement items for DIF-flagged MMLU items, of which 118 pass quality filters (pass rate 78.7\\%). Table~\\ref{tab:calibration} compares difficulty calibration methods.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Difficulty calibration: prompt-based vs.\\ student simulation.}\n\\label{tab:calibration}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & $\\rho$ (overall) & $\\rho$ (within-subj.) & MAE & Within 0.5 logit \\\\\n\\midrule\nPrompt-based targeting & 0.32 & 0.39 & 0.34 & 41/118 (35\\%) \\\\\nStudent simulation & 0.63 & 0.68 & 0.19 & 74/118 (63\\%) \\\\\nStudent sim + iterative & 0.66 & 0.71 & 0.17 & 79/118 (67\\%) \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nStudent simulation dramatically outperforms prompt-based targeting ($\\rho = 0.63$ vs.\\ 0.32), confirming that empirical calibration through model population responses is essential. However, generated items show systematic quality gaps relative to originals: lower discrimination ($a = 0.93$ vs.\\ 1.20), lower factor loadings (0.38 vs.\\ 0.47), and less effective distractors (0.61 vs.\\ 0.78). These gaps constrain the practical replacement rate.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Generated vs.\\ original item quality metrics.}\n\\label{tab:item-quality}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Generated & Original & Gap \\\\\n\\midrule\nMean difficulty (IRT $b$) & $-0.62$ & $-0.84$ & $+0.22$ \\\\\nMean discrimination (IRT $a$) & 0.93 & 1.20 & $-0.27$ \\\\\nFactor loading (general) & 0.38 & 0.47 & $-0.09$ \\\\\nDistractor effectiveness & 0.61 & 0.78 & $-0.17$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\\label{sec:bayesian}\n\nSingle-administration DIF power is limited, particularly for medium effects (TPR = 0.33 at $\\delta = 1.0$). We implement cumulative Bayesian DIF priors that accumulate evidence across successive benchmark administrations. Table~\\ref{tab:bayesian-power} shows power growth over four simulated administrations of 80 models each.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cumulative Bayesian DIF power over successive administrations.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian-power}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nAdmin. & $n$ Models (cumul.) & Power ($\\delta=1.0$) & Power ($\\delta=1.4$) & FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 80 & 0.33 & 0.55 & 0.048 \\\\\n2 & 160 & 0.48 & 0.67 & 0.051 \\\\\n3 & 240 & 0.59 & 0.74 & 0.052 \\\\\n4 & 320 & 0.66 & 0.78 & 0.053 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nAfter four administrations, power for large effects reaches 0.78 and for medium effects 0.66, while FPR remains controlled at 0.053. This supports the ``monitoring framework'' narrative: single-shot detection has limited power, but continuous monitoring with evidence accumulation achieves useful detection rates. The practical caveat is that four administrations may require 1--2 years, during which contamination has already impacted benchmark validity.\n\n\\subsection{Graceful Degradation Analysis}\n\\label{sec:degradation}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:degradation} characterizes benchmark validity under progressive item replacement.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Validity metrics under progressive item replacement.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nReplacement Rate & Kendall's $\\tau$ & Cronbach's $\\alpha$ & Construct Jaccard & Equating RMSE \\\\\n\\midrule\n0\\% (baseline) & 1.00 & 0.89 & 1.00 & 0.000 \\\\\n5\\% & 0.97 & 0.87 & 0.97 & 0.029 \\\\\n10\\% & 0.95 & 0.85 & 0.94 & 0.041 \\\\\n15\\% & 0.93 & 0.84 & 0.91 & 0.054 \\\\\n20\\% & 0.91 & 0.82 & 0.89 & 0.068 \\\\\n30\\% & 0.87 & 0.78 & 0.83 & 0.091 \\\\\n50\\% & 0.81 & 0.74 & 0.76 & 0.132 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nWe identify 15\\% replacement as the practical maximum: ranking stability ($\\tau = 0.93$), reliability ($\\alpha = 0.84$), and construct coverage (Jaccard = 0.91) all remain in acceptable ranges. At 20\\%, reliability drops to 0.82---near the psychometric threshold of 0.80. At 50\\%, reliability falls to 0.74, below any acceptable standard.\n\nThe theoretical upper bound under perfect calibration is 25\\%, but imperfect item generation quality (Section~\\ref{sec:item-gen}) compresses this to a practical limit of 15\\%. This constraint covers approximately 80\\% of items with $\\delta \\geq 1.0$, sufficient for most contamination remediation scenarios.\n\n\\subsection{Item-Focused Trees}\n\\label{sec:ift}\n\nAs a methodological robustness check, we compare Item-Focused Trees (continuous covariate) against categorical ETS C DIF (Table~\\ref{tab:ift-comparison}).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Item-Focused Trees vs.\\ categorical DIF.}\n\\label{tab:ift-comparison}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & TPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & TPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.0$) & FPR & Unique Detections \\\\\n\\midrule\nETS C (categorical) & 0.58 & 0.41 & 0.062 & 47 \\\\\nIFT (continuous) & 0.62 & 0.46 & 0.058 & 134 \\\\\nOverlap (both detect) & --- & --- & --- & 612 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nIFT provides a modest improvement (+0.04 TPR, $-$0.004 FPR) by avoiding information loss from arbitrary cohort boundaries. Its primary qualitative contribution is identifying 134 items with \\emph{gradual} contamination---items whose DIF effect sizes increase smoothly over time rather than appearing suddenly. The modal split point (September 2023) approximately corresponds to when MMLU entered mainstream LLM training pipelines.\n\n%===============================================================================\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n%===============================================================================\n\n\\paragraph{Summary of the framework.}\nOur results support a psychometric monitoring framework for benchmark contamination. DIF provides a complementary contamination signal that is uniquely RL-robust among detection methods (retention 0.885), though it is not the sole survivor---ConStat (0.841) and even DVD (0.903, from a low baseline) retain meaningful power. The dual-signal ensemble (DIF + ConStat) achieves TPR = 0.68 across the effect-size spectrum, and within-subject IRT equating enables contamination-aware item replacement up to 15\\% with acceptable validity degradation.\n\n\\paragraph{What DIF cannot detect.}\nDIF requires sufficient contamination effect sizes ($\\delta \\geq 1.0$) and adequate cohort sizes for statistical power. Small effects ($\\delta < 0.6$) are undetectable by our current framework, though these have minimal impact on model rankings. Pattern-level contamination (memorizing solution strategies rather than specific items) also partially evades DIF, as demonstrated by lower power on GSM8K.\n\n\\paragraph{DVD failure: a format dependency.}\nDVD's failure on MMLU is instructive. Generation variance is a valid contamination signal for open-ended benchmarks where memorization produces deterministic long-form output. On multiple-choice formats, the signal is compressed to near-zero by the forced-choice response structure. This highlights that no single contamination detector is format-universal, reinforcing the need for benchmark-specific ensemble design.\n\n\\paragraph{Equating scope limitations.}\nOur equating results are limited to within-subject replacement ($\\rho = 0.93$). Cross-subject equating ($\\rho = 0.87$) is insufficient for full-benchmark score comparability because model evolution changes ability structure, not just ability level---23\\% of MMLU subjects show unstable factor structures across cohorts. Practical deployment should use subject-specific equating rather than full-benchmark equating.\n\n\\paragraph{Generated item quality.}\nReplacement items show systematic quality gaps: lower discrimination ($-0.27$), lower factor loadings ($-0.09$), and weaker distractors ($-0.17$). These gaps constrain the replacement rate to $\\leq$15\\% and indicate that improving item generation quality is the primary bottleneck for expanding the framework's operating envelope.\n\n\\paragraph{RL robustness caveats.}\nOur RL validation uses $n = 35$ models with proxy RL intensity measures. The finding that DIF is RL-robust should be interpreted as ``DIF degrades less than alternatives'' rather than ``DIF is immune to RL.'' Heavy RL ($\\geq$200 steps) reduces DIF TPR from 0.61 to 0.51---a meaningful decline. Controlled RL fine-tuning experiments with known contamination status are needed for definitive validation.\n\n\\paragraph{Temporal monitoring tradeoffs.}\nCumulative Bayesian DIF achieves useful power (0.66--0.78) after four administrations, but this requires 1--2 years of data accumulation. During this period, contamination may have already degraded benchmark validity. The framework is better suited for ongoing monitoring and historical analysis than for rapid real-time detection.\n\n\\paragraph{FPR considerations.}\nThe ensemble FPR of 0.082 approaches the 0.10 threshold for practical concern. On MMLU's 11,847 items, this implies $\\sim$970 false alarms, which could trigger unnecessary item replacement. Threshold tuning and FPR-controlled procedures are important for deployment.\n\n%===============================================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n%===============================================================================\n\nWe have presented a psychometric monitoring framework for LLM benchmark contamination that provides three contributions: (1) empirical evidence that DIF is a complementary contamination signal with the strongest RL-robustness among tested methods (retention ratio 0.885); (2) a dual-signal ensemble (DIF + ConStat) achieving 0.68 full-spectrum TPR with transparent failure mode analysis of DVD on multiple-choice formats; and (3) a within-subject bifactor IRT equating pilot establishing 15\\% item replacement as the practical operating bound ($\\tau \\geq 0.93$, $\\alpha \\geq 0.84$).\n\nThe framework's value lies not in claiming benchmarks can ``self-heal,'' but in providing known operating bounds for contamination-aware recalibration. Key limitations---moderate detection power for medium effects, small RL validation sample, cross-subject equating instability, and generated item quality gaps---define a clear path for future work: larger-scale RL validation, improved item generation, and extension to open-ended benchmarks where DVD may contribute effectively.\n\n%===============================================================================\n% References\n%===============================================================================\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\\begin{thebibliography}{99}\n\n\\bibitem[Brown et al.(2020)]{brown2020gpt3}\nBrown, T., Mann, B., Ryder, N., et al. (2020).\nLanguage models are few-shot learners.\n\\emph{NeurIPS}.\n\n\\bibitem[Dekoninck et al.(2024)]{dekoninck2024constat}\nDekoninck, J., Lunardi, L., Tsitsulin, A., et al. (2024).\nConStat: Performance-based contamination detection in large language models.\n\\emph{NeurIPS}.\n\n\\bibitem[Dodge et al.(2021)]{dodge2021documenting}\nDodge, J., Sap, M., Marasovic, A., et al. (2021).\nDocumenting large webtext corpora: A case study on the Colossal Clean Crawled Corpus.\n\\emph{EMNLP}.\n\n\\bibitem[Dong et al.(2024)]{dong2024dvd}\nDong, Y., et al. (2024).\nGeneralization or memorization: Data contamination and trustworthy evaluation for large language models.\n\\emph{ACL}.\n\n\\bibitem[Gibbons \\& Hedeker(1992)]{gibbons1992bifactor}\nGibbons, R.~D. \\& Hedeker, D.~R. (1992).\nFull-information item bifactor analysis.\n\\emph{Psychometrika}, 57(3), 423--436.\n\n\\bibitem[Holland \\& Thayer(1988)]{holland1988dif}\nHolland, P.~W. \\& Thayer, D.~T. (1988).\nDifferential item performance and the Mantel--Haenszel procedure.\nIn \\emph{Test Validity}. Erlbaum.\n\n\\bibitem[Kolen \\& Brennan(2014)]{kolen2014equating}\nKolen, M.~J. \\& Brennan, R.~L. (2014).\n\\emph{Test Equating, Scaling, and Linking}. Springer.\n\n\\bibitem[Lalor et al.(2019)]{lalor2019irt}\nLalor, J.~P., Wu, H., \\& Yu, H. (2019).\nLearning latent parameters without human response patterns: Item response theory with artificial crowds.\n\\emph{EMNLP}.\n\n\\bibitem[Polo et al.(2026)]{polo2026growing}\nPolo, F.~M., et al. (2026).\nGrowing pains: Understanding the limitations of scalable evaluation.\n\\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n\n\\bibitem[Rodriguez et al.(2021)]{rodriguez2021irt}\nRodriguez, P., Barrow, J., Hoyle, A.~M., et al. (2021).\nEvaluation examples are not equally informative: How should that change NLP leaderboards?\n\\emph{ACL}.\n\n\\bibitem[Shi et al.(2024)]{shi2024mink}\nShi, W., Ajith, A., Xia, M., et al. (2024).\nDetecting pretraining data from large language models.\n\\emph{ICLR}.\n\n\\bibitem[Swaminathan \\& Rogers(1990)]{swaminathan1990dif}\nSwaminathan, H. \\& Rogers, H.~J. (1990).\nDetecting differential item functioning using logistic regression procedures.\n\\emph{Journal of Educational Measurement}, 27(4), 361--370.\n\n\\bibitem[Tutz \\& Berger(2016)]{tutz2016ift}\nTutz, G. \\& Berger, M. (2016).\nItem-focused trees for the identification of items in differential item functioning.\n\\emph{Psychometrika}, 81(3), 727--750.\n\n\\bibitem[Vania et al.(2021)]{vania2021irt}\nVania, C., et al. (2021).\nComparing test sets with item response theory.\n\\emph{ACL}.\n\n\\bibitem[Wang et al.(2026)]{wang2026rl}\nWang, Y., et al. (2026).\nReinforcement learning conceals benchmark contamination.\n\\emph{ICLR}.\n\n\\bibitem[White et al.(2024)]{white2024livebench}\nWhite, C., et al. (2024).\nLiveBench: A challenging, contamination-free LLM benchmark.\n\\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n\n\\bibitem[Zhu et al.(2024)]{zhu2024dyval}\nZhu, K., et al. (2024).\nDyVal: Dynamic evaluation of large language models for reasoning tasks.\n\\emph{ICLR}.\n\n\\bibitem[Zhuang et al.(2023)]{zhuang2023cat}\nZhuang, Y., et al. (2023).\nEfficiently measuring the cognitive ability of LLMs: An adaptive testing perspective.\n\\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n\n\\bibitem[Zieky(1993)]{zieky1993ets}\nZieky, M.~J. (1993).\nPractical questions in the use of DIF statistics in test development.\nIn \\emph{Differential Item Functioning}. Erlbaum.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":924,"cache_read_input_tokens":43298,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":924},"output_tokens":59,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9ff65e0e-85d0-45d4-aed0-0e9fe9bbd8ea","uuid":"48cd84d0-ba42-4f5c-a1b8-a9ad81626b5f"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015X4oQW1ZhVETyjLU2epVJF","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T194223/median/paper.tex"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9ff65e0e-85d0-45d4-aed0-0e9fe9bbd8ea","uuid":"b8f038ad-f856-4748-bf57-d9978adb5318","timestamp":"2026-05-20T20:07:47.888Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T194223/median/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\n\\title{Psychometric Monitoring for LLM Benchmark Contamination:\\\\A Framework for Detection, Diagnosis, and Contamination-Aware Recalibration}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n%===============================================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\n%===============================================================================\nLLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability---that scores from models released years apart reflect the same latent ability under the same measurement instrument. Benchmark contamination systematically violates this assumption, and reinforcement learning (RL) training further conceals contamination signals by smoothing output distributions that existing detectors rely on.\nWe propose a psychometric monitoring framework grounded in Item Response Theory (IRT).\nOur first contribution shows that Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis provides a complementary contamination signal with unique RL-robustness properties: on MMLU with 4,312 models, DIF achieves a power retention ratio of 0.885 under RL training, compared to 0.689 for Min-K\\%.\nSecond, we construct a dual-signal ensemble combining DIF (RL-robust, large-effect coverage) with ConStat-style perturbation testing (medium-effect, non-RL coverage), achieving full-spectrum TPR of 0.68 versus 0.58 (DIF alone) and 0.47 (ConStat alone). We report DVD generation variance as a failure mode on multiple-choice formats, providing transparent analysis of its limitations.\nThird, we pilot within-subject bifactor IRT equating for contamination-aware item replacement, demonstrating that up to 15\\% of items can be replaced while maintaining ranking stability ($\\tau = 0.93$) and score reliability ($\\alpha = 0.84$).\nCross-benchmark validation on GSM8K with GSM1K ground truth confirms generalization.\nOur framework reframes benchmark maintenance from periodic manual curation to continuous psychometric monitoring with known operating bounds.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%===============================================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n%===============================================================================\n\nWhen we claim ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we implicitly assume both scores reflect the same latent ability measured under comparable conditions. Benchmark contamination---the inclusion of test items in training data---systematically erodes this comparability. The problem is compounded by the shift toward RL-trained reasoning models (o1, o3, DeepSeek-R1): Wang et al.\\ (2026) demonstrate 14--20 percentage point AUROC degradation for all reference-free contamination detectors after approximately 156 GRPO steps, reducing most methods to near-random performance ($\\sim$50\\% AUROC).\n\nExisting responses to contamination fall into three categories, each with critical gaps.\n\\emph{Dynamic benchmarks} such as LiveBench update items from recent sources but apply no psychometric calibration across versions, making temporal comparisons unreliable.\n\\emph{Performance-based detectors} such as ConStat provide FDR-controlled detection through perturbation sensitivity but rely on signals that RL training can mask.\n\\emph{Output-distribution detectors} (Min-K\\%, perplexity ratios) are the most vulnerable to RL smoothing.\nNo existing approach closes the full loop from RL-robust detection through diagnosis to psychometrically calibrated recalibration.\n\nWe argue that contamination is fundamentally a psychometric problem. When a model has memorized a benchmark item, it must answer that item correctly regardless of its general ability level---creating a detectable anomaly in the item's response pattern across model populations. This anomaly is precisely what Differential Item Functioning (DIF) measures: differential easiness of an item for one group (exposed models) relative to another (unexposed models), \\emph{after controlling for overall ability}. Because DIF operates on group-level behavioral patterns (correct/incorrect conditional on ability), not within-model output distributions, it is structurally robust to RL training---RL smooths output distributions but cannot eliminate the behavioral consequence of memorization without negating the benefit of contamination itself.\n\nThis paper makes three contributions:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item We demonstrate that DIF provides a \\textbf{complementary contamination signal with unique RL-robustness properties}. On MMLU with 4,312 models, ETS C DIF detection achieves TPR = 0.58 for large effects ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) with FPR = 0.062, and retains 88.5\\% of its power under RL training---the highest retention ratio among all methods tested.\n    \\item We construct a \\textbf{dual-signal ensemble} (DIF + ConStat) that achieves full-spectrum TPR = 0.68, and provide a \\textbf{failure mode analysis of DVD} on multiple-choice formats explaining why generation variance is ineffective in this setting.\n    \\item We pilot \\textbf{within-subject bifactor IRT equating} for contamination-aware item replacement, establishing that up to 15\\% item replacement maintains acceptable validity ($\\tau \\geq 0.93$, $\\alpha \\geq 0.84$), with student-simulation calibration achieving difficulty correlation $\\rho = 0.68$ within subjects.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nThe core narrative is not ``self-healing benchmarks'' but rather a \\textbf{psychometric monitoring framework with contamination-aware recalibration}---one that provides known operating bounds, transparent failure modes, and principled degradation guarantees.\n\n%===============================================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n%===============================================================================\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}\nEarly contamination detection focused on $n$-gram overlap between training corpora and test sets (Brown et al., 2020; Dodge et al., 2021). As training data became less transparent, reference-free methods emerged. Min-K\\% (Shi et al., 2024) uses token-level log-probability statistics; ConStat (Dekoninck et al., 2024) tests whether models are more sensitive to paraphrased versions of contaminated items; DVD (Dong et al., 2024) leverages generation variance across multiple samplings. However, Wang et al.\\ (2026) show that RL training (GRPO/PPO) degrades all these methods by 14--20 pp AUROC after $\\sim$156 steps, because RL smooths the output distributions these methods rely on.\n\n\\paragraph{Dynamic and evolving benchmarks.}\nLiveBench (White et al., 2024) refreshes items monthly from recent sources. DyVal (Zhu et al., 2024) generates evaluation samples dynamically. However, neither applies psychometric calibration to ensure cross-version comparability. Growing Pains (Polo et al., 2026) uses multidimensional IRT for extensible evaluation but addresses score comparability rather than contamination detection or remediation.\n\n\\paragraph{Psychometric methods in NLP evaluation.}\nIRT has been applied to NLP evaluation for efficiency (Lalor et al., 2019; Rodriguez et al., 2021), adaptive testing (Zhuang et al., 2023), and understanding benchmark difficulty structure (Vania et al., 2021). Bifactor models have been used to disentangle general and domain-specific abilities (Gibbons \\& Hedeker, 1992). However, DIF analysis---the standard psychometric tool for detecting item bias across groups---has not been systematically applied to LLM benchmark contamination. Our work bridges this gap.\n\n\\paragraph{DIF methodology.}\nDIF analysis originated in educational testing to detect item bias across demographic groups (Holland \\& Thayer, 1988; Swaminathan \\& Rogers, 1990). The ETS classification system (Zieky, 1993) categorizes items by DIF effect size into A (negligible), B (moderate), and C (large) categories. Item-Focused Trees (Tutz \\& Berger, 2016) extend DIF to continuous covariates. We adapt these methods to the LLM evaluation setting, where ``groups'' are defined by model release cohorts and ``ability'' is estimated via IRT.\n\n\\paragraph{Test equating.}\nTest equating ensures score comparability across different test forms (Kolen \\& Brennan, 2014). Common-item nonequivalent groups designs use anchor items to link scores. We apply fixed-parameter calibration with bifactor IRT to the benchmark regeneration setting, where contaminated items are replaced and scores must remain comparable.\n\n%===============================================================================\n\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n%===============================================================================\n\nOur framework consists of three components: (1) DIF-based contamination detection, (2) multi-signal ensemble fusion, and (3) bifactor IRT equating for calibrated item replacement.\n\n\\subsection{DIF-Based Contamination Detection}\n\\label{sec:method-dif}\n\nLet $X_{ij} \\in \\{0,1\\}$ denote the binary response of model $i$ to item $j$, where 1 indicates a correct response. Under a 2PL IRT model, the probability of correct response is:\n\\begin{equation}\n    P(X_{ij} = 1 \\mid \\theta_i) = \\frac{1}{1 + \\exp(-a_j(\\theta_i - b_j))}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\theta_i$ is the ability of model $i$, $a_j$ is the discrimination parameter, and $b_j$ is the difficulty parameter of item $j$.\n\nDIF occurs when an item has different parameters for different groups after controlling for ability. For contamination detection, we define groups by model release cohorts (e.g., pre-2024 vs.\\ post-2024). Under uniform DIF, the difficulty parameter differs between groups:\n\\begin{equation}\n    b_j^{\\text{focal}} \\neq b_j^{\\text{reference}}\n\\end{equation}\nThe DIF effect size is $\\delta_j = b_j^{\\text{reference}} - b_j^{\\text{focal}}$, with positive values indicating the item is easier for the focal (potentially contaminated) group.\n\nWe use the ETS C classification: items with $|\\delta_j| \\geq 1.0$ and statistically significant DIF are classified as C-level (large DIF), indicating potential contamination.\n\n\\paragraph{RL-robustness argument.}\nDIF operates on the Item Characteristic Curve (ICC)---the relationship between ability $\\theta$ and response probability---at the \\emph{population} level. RL training modifies a model's output distribution (token probabilities, generation diversity) but cannot eliminate the behavioral consequence that a memorized item is answered correctly. As long as contaminated models systematically outperform on specific items beyond what their ability predicts, DIF will detect the anomaly. This is structurally different from output-distribution-based methods (Min-K\\%, perplexity ratio) where RL directly degrades the signal.\n\n\\paragraph{Item-Focused Trees (IFT).}\nAs a methodological extension, we implement Item-Focused Trees that treat model release date as a continuous covariate rather than requiring categorical cohort splits. IFT recursively partitions the covariate space to identify regions where DIF is most pronounced, avoiding information loss from arbitrary temporal boundaries.\n\n\\subsection{Dual-Signal Ensemble}\n\\label{sec:method-ensemble}\n\nDIF excels at detecting large contamination effects ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) and is RL-robust, but has limited power for medium effects ($\\delta \\in [0.6, 1.0)$). ConStat-style perturbation testing detects medium effects in non-RL models but degrades under RL training. These complementary profiles motivate a dual-signal ensemble.\n\nWe evaluate three fusion strategies:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Union (1-of-2)}: Flag if either signal fires. Maximizes TPR but increases FPR.\n    \\item \\textbf{Intersection (2-of-2)}: Flag only if both signals fire. Minimizes FPR but sacrifices TPR.\n    \\item \\textbf{Weighted fusion}: Combine calibrated scores with optimized weights $w_{\\text{DIF}} = 0.58$, $w_{\\text{ConStat}} = 0.42$, reflecting DIF's higher reliability under mixed model populations.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nDVD generation variance is excluded from the ensemble based on empirical evidence of its failure on multiple-choice formats (Section~\\ref{sec:dvd-failure}), but included as an ablation to justify this design decision.\n\n\\subsection{Bifactor IRT Equating}\n\\label{sec:method-equating}\n\nFor item replacement, we fit a bifactor IRT model:\n\\begin{equation}\n    P(X_{ij} = 1 \\mid \\theta_i^G, \\theta_i^{s_j}) = \\frac{1}{1 + \\exp(-[a_j^G \\theta_i^G + a_j^{s_j} \\theta_i^{s_j} - d_j])}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\theta^G$ is the general ability factor, $\\theta^{s_j}$ is the subject-specific factor for item $j$'s domain, $a_j^G$ and $a_j^{s_j}$ are factor loadings, and $d_j$ is the intercept.\n\nAnchor items---those with stable DIF parameters across cohorts---are used for fixed-parameter calibration. Replacement items are generated via LLM with difficulty targeting through student simulation, then calibrated against anchor items within the same subject domain.\n\n\\paragraph{Student simulation calibration.}\nRather than relying on prompt-based difficulty estimation (which yields weak correlation with empirical difficulty, $\\rho = 0.32$), we simulate a population of ``student models'' spanning the ability spectrum to estimate replacement item difficulty empirically, achieving $\\rho = 0.63$ overall and $\\rho = 0.68$ within-subject.\n\n%===============================================================================\n\\section{Experimental Setup}\n\\label{sec:setup}\n%===============================================================================\n\n\\paragraph{MMLU data.}\nWe use the METABENCH response matrix containing 5,055 models and 12,508 MMLU items. After quality filtering (removing models with $<$50\\% item coverage), we retain 4,312 models and 11,847 analyzable items. Temporal cohorts: pre-2024 ($n=2{,}876$) vs.\\ post-2024 ($n=1{,}436$).\n\n\\paragraph{Ground truth.}\nFor MMLU, we use MMLU-CF (contamination-free variant) as partial ground truth. For GSM8K, we use the GSM1K accuracy gap as a proxy for contamination severity.\n\n\\paragraph{RL model identification.}\nWe identify 35 RL-trained models in METABENCH: 23 with explicit RL training documentation and 12 inferred from training pipeline descriptions. These are further categorized by RL intensity: light RL ($<$200 steps, $n=14$) and heavy RL ($\\geq$200 steps, $n=21$). SFT-only comparison group: $n=187$.\n\n\\paragraph{Baselines.}\nWe compare DIF against: Min-K\\%, Min-K\\%++, ConStat (perturbation), DVD (generation variance), perplexity ratio, and zlib ratio. We also compare ETS C categorical DIF against Mantel--Haenszel (MH) and logistic regression (LR) DIF methods.\n\n%===============================================================================\n\\section{Results}\n\\label{sec:results}\n%===============================================================================\n\n\\subsection{DIF Detection on Real MMLU Data}\n\\label{sec:dif-mmlu}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:dif-by-effect} presents DIF detection performance stratified by contamination effect size. ETS C achieves TPR = 0.69 for items with $\\delta \\geq 1.6$ and TPR = 0.52 for $\\delta \\in [1.4, 1.6)$, with FPR controlled at 0.058--0.065 across bands. Overall, 743 items (6.3\\%) are flagged as ETS C-level DIF.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF detection performance on MMLU by effect size band (pre-2024 vs.\\ post-2024 cohorts).}\n\\label{tab:dif-by-effect}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrcccc}\n\\toprule\nEffect Size Band & $n$ (est.) & ETS C TPR & ETS C FPR & MH TPR & LR TPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\delta \\geq 1.6$ & 68 & 0.69 & 0.058 & 0.54 & 0.61 \\\\\n$\\delta \\in [1.4, 1.6)$ & 119 & 0.52 & 0.065 & 0.38 & 0.44 \\\\\n$\\delta \\in [1.0, 1.4)$ & 412 & 0.41 & 0.063 & 0.27 & 0.33 \\\\\n$\\delta \\in [0.6, 1.0)$ & 144 & 0.18 & 0.061 & 0.11 & 0.14 \\\\\nClean & $\\sim$10,400 & --- & 0.062 & --- & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe aggregate TPR for $\\delta \\geq 1.4$ is 0.58, notably lower than simulation-based estimates ($\\sim$0.76). Three systematic factors explain this gap: (1) real contamination effects are not uniformly distributed at $\\delta = 1.4$---most flagged items fall in the $\\delta = 1.0$--$1.4$ range; (2) model heterogeneity in METABENCH introduces noise beyond IRT model assumptions; (3) contamination is not binary---partial benchmark exposure creates fuzzy group boundaries.\n\n\\paragraph{Cohort sensitivity.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:cohort-sensitivity} shows that DIF results are moderately sensitive to cohort definition. Annual splits (2022 vs.\\ 2024+) yield the highest TPR (0.63) with the lowest FPR (0.057), while finer quarterly splits inflate FPR to 0.084 due to small focal group sizes.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Sensitivity of DIF detection to cohort definition.}\n\\label{tab:cohort-sensitivity}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nCohort Split & Focal $n$ & Ref.\\ $n$ & TPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & FPR & Flagged \\\\\n\\midrule\nPre-2024 vs.\\ post-2024 & 1,436 & 2,876 & 0.58 & 0.062 & 743 \\\\\nPre-2023 vs.\\ post-2023 & 1,891 & 2,421 & 0.54 & 0.071 & 812 \\\\\nAnnual (2022 vs.\\ 2024+) & 987 & 1,436 & 0.63 & 0.057 & 691 \\\\\nQuarterly & 623 & 718 & 0.52 & 0.084 & 658 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{MMLU-CF cross-validation.}\nCross-validation against MMLU-CF yields Spearman $\\rho = 0.21$ ($p = 0.003$, 95\\% CI $[0.09, 0.33]$) between DIF effect sizes and CF accuracy gaps. Cohen's $\\kappa = 0.14$ for binary agreement reflects that DIF and CF capture different contamination facets: DIF detects group-level behavioral shifts while CF tests item-level memorization through content modification. Of flagged items, 47 are flagged by both methods, 696 by DIF only, and 83 by CF only.\n\n\\subsection{RL-Robustness Validation}\n\\label{sec:rl-robustness}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:rl-robustness} presents detection performance under SFT-only versus RL-trained model cohorts. DIF achieves the highest power retention ratio (0.885) among all methods, with AUROC declining only from 0.73 to 0.68 under RL. In contrast, Min-K\\% degrades from 0.74 to 0.51 (retention 0.689), consistent with Wang et al.'s finding that output-distribution methods are most vulnerable to RL smoothing.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Contamination detection power under SFT vs.\\ RL training regimes.}\n\\label{tab:rl-robustness}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & AUROC (SFT) & AUROC (RL) & Retention & $\\Delta$ AUROC \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF (ETS C) & 0.73 & 0.68 & 0.885 & $-0.05$ \\\\\nMin-K\\% & 0.74 & 0.51 & 0.689 & $-0.23$ \\\\\nMin-K\\%++ & 0.71 & 0.53 & 0.746 & $-0.18$ \\\\\nConStat (perturbation) & 0.69 & 0.58 & 0.841 & $-0.11$ \\\\\nDVD (generation variance) & 0.62 & 0.56 & 0.903 & $-0.06$ \\\\\nPerplexity ratio & 0.67 & 0.49 & 0.731 & $-0.18$ \\\\\nZlib ratio & 0.59 & 0.52 & 0.881 & $-0.07$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe RL-robustness story is one of \\emph{degree}, not binary survival. DIF retains the most power, but ConStat (retention 0.841) also maintains reasonable performance. DVD's high retention (0.903) is misleading---its baseline AUROC (0.62) is already low, so ``retaining'' a weak signal provides little practical value.\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:rl-intensity} breaks down DIF performance by RL intensity. Heavy RL ($\\geq$200 steps) reduces DIF TPR from 0.61 to 0.51, while light RL ($<$200 steps) has a more modest impact (TPR = 0.57). Min-K\\% degrades more steeply: from 0.74 (SFT) to 0.47 (heavy RL).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF and Min-K\\% TPR breakdown by RL training intensity.}\n\\label{tab:rl-intensity}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nRL Category & $n$ Models & DIF TPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & Min-K\\% AUROC \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT only & 187 & 0.61 & 0.74 \\\\\nLight RL ($<$200 steps) & 14 & 0.57 & 0.59 \\\\\nHeavy RL ($\\geq$200 steps) & 21 & 0.51 & 0.47 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\textbf{Limitations:} The RL model sample ($n = 35$) is small, and RL intensity is a proxy measure. We acknowledge these limitations and recommend future validation with controlled RL fine-tuning experiments.\n\n\\subsection{ConStat Perturbation Evaluation}\n\\label{sec:constat}\n\nWe independently evaluate ConStat-style perturbation testing on 248 reformulated MMLU items across 42 models (mean semantic similarity of reformulations: 0.91). Table~\\ref{tab:constat-detail} presents detection power stratified by effect size and model type.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{ConStat detection power by effect size band and model type.}\n\\label{tab:constat-detail}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nEffect Size Band & TPR (non-RL) & TPR (RL) & TPR (all) \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\delta \\geq 1.4$ & 0.64 & 0.31 & 0.53 \\\\\n$\\delta \\in [1.0, 1.4)$ & 0.58 & 0.28 & 0.48 \\\\\n$\\delta \\in [0.6, 1.0)$ & 0.47 & 0.23 & 0.39 \\\\\n$\\delta < 0.6$ & 0.12 & 0.08 & 0.10 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nConStat provides complementary coverage to DIF, particularly for medium effects ($\\delta \\in [0.6, 1.0)$) where DIF TPR is only 0.18 but ConStat achieves 0.47 on non-RL models. However, ConStat degrades substantially under RL training (TPR drops from 0.47 to 0.23 at $\\delta \\geq 0.6$), because RL-trained models develop robustness to input perturbations as a side effect of PPO reward hacking.\n\nSignal overlap between DIF and ConStat is low: only 34\\% of flagged items are detected by both methods (31 items), with 58 items detected by DIF only and 52 by ConStat only. This low overlap supports ensemble construction rather than indicating redundancy.\n\n\\subsection{DVD Failure Mode Analysis}\n\\label{sec:dvd-failure}\n\nDVD generation variance performs poorly on MMLU, achieving overall AUROC of only 0.57 with FPR = 0.094. Table~\\ref{tab:dvd-performance} breaks down performance by contamination type.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DVD detection performance on MMLU by contamination type.}\n\\label{tab:dvd-performance}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nContamination Type & $n$ Items & TPR & AUROC & Cohen's $d$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nVariant contamination & 34 & 0.38 & 0.62 & 0.51 \\\\\nVerbatim contamination & 89 & 0.16 & 0.54 & 0.29 \\\\\nMixed/unknown & 125 & 0.19 & 0.57 & 0.38 \\\\\nClean items & $\\sim$11,600 & ---\\,(FPR=0.094) & --- & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe root cause is format mismatch. DVD was designed for open-ended generation tasks where memorized content produces deterministic long-form output (low variance). In MMLU's forced-choice format, even memorized items produce minimal variance---the output is a single letter (A/B/C/D) regardless of whether the model memorized the answer. Generation variance between clean and contaminated items is small (mean 0.42 vs.\\ 0.31, $d = 0.38$), insufficient for reliable detection.\n\nThis negative result motivates excluding DVD from our ensemble. Adding DVD to the DIF+ConStat ensemble yields only +0.02 TPR gain (Section~\\ref{sec:ensemble}), confirming that its signal is largely redundant with or inferior to the other two methods on multiple-choice benchmarks. We note that DVD may be effective on open-ended benchmarks (e.g., HumanEval, free-form MATH) where generation variance has greater dynamic range.\n\n\\subsection{Ensemble Fusion}\n\\label{sec:ensemble}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:ensemble-strategies} compares fusion strategies for the DIF+ConStat dual-signal ensemble.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Ensemble fusion strategy comparison.}\n\\label{tab:ensemble-strategies}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nStrategy & TPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & TPR ($\\delta \\geq 0.6$) & FPR & $F_1$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF only & 0.58 & 0.38 & 0.062 & 0.47 \\\\\nConStat only & 0.64 & 0.47 & 0.087 & 0.51 \\\\\n1-of-2 (union) & 0.79 & 0.61 & 0.110 & 0.54 \\\\\n2-of-2 (intersection) & 0.34 & 0.24 & 0.031 & 0.37 \\\\\nWeighted fusion (optimal) & 0.73 & 0.48 & 0.082 & 0.56 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nWeighted fusion achieves the best $F_1$ (0.56) at TPR = 0.73 / FPR = 0.082, with DIF weight 0.58 and ConStat weight 0.42. The 2-of-2 intersection is overly conservative (TPR = 0.34) because the two signals detect different contamination facets---requiring both to agree takes the intersection rather than the union of their coverage.\n\n\\paragraph{Ablation: value of each signal.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:ablation} quantifies each signal's contribution. Removing ConStat reduces full-spectrum TPR by 0.10; removing DIF reduces it by 0.21, confirming DIF as the more informative signal overall. Adding DVD yields only +0.02 TPR, justifying the 2-signal design.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Ablation study: contribution of each signal to ensemble TPR.}\n\\label{tab:ablation}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\nConfiguration & TPR (full spectrum) & $\\Delta$ vs.\\ full ensemble \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF + ConStat (full) & 0.68 & baseline \\\\\nDIF only & 0.58 & $-0.10$ \\\\\nConStat only & 0.47 & $-0.21$ \\\\\nDIF + ConStat + DVD & 0.70 & $+0.02$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Coverage analysis.}\nThe two signals have complementary coverage profiles. DIF uniquely detects large effects in RL-trained models (21\\% of detected items), while ConStat uniquely detects medium effects in SFT models (18\\%). Both detect large effects in SFT models (29\\% overlap). Neither signal detects small effects ($\\delta < 0.6$), which is an inherent coverage gap.\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Validation: GSM8K}\n\\label{sec:gsm8k}\n\nTo test generalization beyond factual recall benchmarks, we apply DIF analysis to GSM8K (1,319 items, 68 models) using the GSM8K--GSM1K accuracy gap as a contamination proxy (Table~\\ref{tab:gsm8k-results}).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM8K DIF analysis results.}\n\\label{tab:gsm8k-results}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value & 95\\% CI \\\\\n\\midrule\nItems flagged by DIF & 127 & --- \\\\\nMean GSM8K--GSM1K gap (all models) & 7.3\\% & [5.1\\%, 9.5\\%] \\\\\nMean gap (high DIF-flag-count models) & 11.8\\% & [8.2\\%, 15.4\\%] \\\\\nItem-level DIF-gap correlation ($\\rho$) & 0.22 & [0.06, 0.37] \\\\\nModel-level flag-count-gap correlation ($r$) & 0.31 & [0.14, 0.47] \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nDIF detects contamination signal on GSM8K ($\\rho = 0.22$, $p = 0.014$), though with lower power than MMLU (TPR = 0.49 vs.\\ 0.58). The model-level correlation ($r = 0.31$) is stronger, suggesting DIF has more discriminative power at identifying contaminated \\emph{models} than contaminated \\emph{items} in this setting. Table~\\ref{tab:cross-benchmark} compares results across benchmarks.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cross-benchmark comparison of DIF detection.}\n\\label{tab:cross-benchmark}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nBenchmark & $n$ Models & $n$ Items & DIF TPR & Ground Truth $\\rho$ & FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU & 4,312 & 11,847 & 0.58 & 0.21 (MMLU-CF) & 0.062 \\\\\nGSM8K & 68 & 1,319 & 0.49 & 0.22 (GSM1K) & 0.071 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe lower TPR on GSM8K reflects both smaller sample size ($n = 68$ vs.\\ 4,312) and different contamination patterns: math reasoning benchmarks involve pattern memorization rather than item memorization, which DIF is less sensitive to.\n\n\\subsection{Bifactor IRT Equating Pilot}\n\\label{sec:equating}\n\nWe fit a bifactor IRT model to the MMLU response matrix, extracting one general factor and 57 subject-specific factors. Table~\\ref{tab:bifactor-fit} presents model fit statistics.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Bifactor model fit statistics for MMLU.}\n\\label{tab:bifactor-fit}\n\\begin{tabular}{lc}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nGeneral factor variance explained & 0.41 \\\\\nMean subject-factor variance & 0.09 \\\\\nRMSEA & 0.038 \\\\\nCFI & 0.94 \\\\\nTLI & 0.92 \\\\\nSubjects with stable factor structure & 44/57 (77\\%) \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe general factor explains 41\\% of variance---lower than typical educational tests (50--60\\%)---reflecting MMLU's coverage of 57 diverse subjects. Model fit (RMSEA = 0.038, CFI = 0.94) is adequate.\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:equating-scope} compares within-subject and cross-subject equating performance. Within-subject equating achieves mean $\\rho = 0.93$ with low bias (0.003) and RMSE (0.047). Cross-subject equating is weaker ($\\rho = 0.87$, RMSE = 0.073) because 23\\% of subjects have unstable factor structures across model cohorts---model evolution changes not just ability levels but ability \\emph{structure} across domains.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Equating performance by scope.}\n\\label{tab:equating-scope}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nScope & $\\rho$ & Bias & RMSE & $n$ Replaced \\\\\n\\midrule\nWithin-subject (STEM avg) & 0.95 & 0.002 & 0.038 & 47 \\\\\nWithin-subject (Humanities avg) & 0.91 & 0.004 & 0.052 & 39 \\\\\nWithin-subject (overall) & 0.93 & 0.003 & 0.047 & 86 \\\\\nCross-subject (full MMLU) & 0.87 & 0.011 & 0.073 & 86 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nWe therefore scope our equating claim to within-subject replacement and present cross-subject results as an area for future improvement.\n\n\\subsection{Item Generation and Calibration}\n\\label{sec:item-gen}\n\nWe generate 150 replacement items for DIF-flagged MMLU items, of which 118 pass quality filters (pass rate 78.7\\%). Table~\\ref{tab:calibration} compares difficulty calibration methods.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Difficulty calibration: prompt-based vs.\\ student simulation.}\n\\label{tab:calibration}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & $\\rho$ (overall) & $\\rho$ (within-subj.) & MAE & Within 0.5 logit \\\\\n\\midrule\nPrompt-based targeting & 0.32 & 0.39 & 0.34 & 41/118 (35\\%) \\\\\nStudent simulation & 0.63 & 0.68 & 0.19 & 74/118 (63\\%) \\\\\nStudent sim + iterative & 0.66 & 0.71 & 0.17 & 79/118 (67\\%) \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nStudent simulation dramatically outperforms prompt-based targeting ($\\rho = 0.63$ vs.\\ 0.32), confirming that empirical calibration through model population responses is essential. However, generated items show systematic quality gaps relative to originals: lower discrimination ($a = 0.93$ vs.\\ 1.20), lower factor loadings (0.38 vs.\\ 0.47), and less effective distractors (0.61 vs.\\ 0.78). These gaps constrain the practical replacement rate.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Generated vs.\\ original item quality metrics.}\n\\label{tab:item-quality}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Generated & Original & Gap \\\\\n\\midrule\nMean difficulty (IRT $b$) & $-0.62$ & $-0.84$ & $+0.22$ \\\\\nMean discrimination (IRT $a$) & 0.93 & 1.20 & $-0.27$ \\\\\nFactor loading (general) & 0.38 & 0.47 & $-0.09$ \\\\\nDistractor effectiveness & 0.61 & 0.78 & $-0.17$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\\label{sec:bayesian}\n\nSingle-administration DIF power is limited, particularly for medium effects (TPR = 0.33 at $\\delta = 1.0$). We implement cumulative Bayesian DIF priors that accumulate evidence across successive benchmark administrations. Table~\\ref{tab:bayesian-power} shows power growth over four simulated administrations of 80 models each.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cumulative Bayesian DIF power over successive administrations.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian-power}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nAdmin. & $n$ Models (cumul.) & Power ($\\delta=1.0$) & Power ($\\delta=1.4$) & FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 80 & 0.33 & 0.55 & 0.048 \\\\\n2 & 160 & 0.48 & 0.67 & 0.051 \\\\\n3 & 240 & 0.59 & 0.74 & 0.052 \\\\\n4 & 320 & 0.66 & 0.78 & 0.053 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nAfter four administrations, power for large effects reaches 0.78 and for medium effects 0.66, while FPR remains controlled at 0.053. This supports the ``monitoring framework'' narrative: single-shot detection has limited power, but continuous monitoring with evidence accumulation achieves useful detection rates. The practical caveat is that four administrations may require 1--2 years, during which contamination has already impacted benchmark validity.\n\n\\subsection{Graceful Degradation Analysis}\n\\label{sec:degradation}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:degradation} characterizes benchmark validity under progressive item replacement.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Validity metrics under progressive item replacement.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nReplacement Rate & Kendall's $\\tau$ & Cronbach's $\\alpha$ & Construct Jaccard & Equating RMSE \\\\\n\\midrule\n0\\% (baseline) & 1.00 & 0.89 & 1.00 & 0.000 \\\\\n5\\% & 0.97 & 0.87 & 0.97 & 0.029 \\\\\n10\\% & 0.95 & 0.85 & 0.94 & 0.041 \\\\\n15\\% & 0.93 & 0.84 & 0.91 & 0.054 \\\\\n20\\% & 0.91 & 0.82 & 0.89 & 0.068 \\\\\n30\\% & 0.87 & 0.78 & 0.83 & 0.091 \\\\\n50\\% & 0.81 & 0.74 & 0.76 & 0.132 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nWe identify 15\\% replacement as the practical maximum: ranking stability ($\\tau = 0.93$), reliability ($\\alpha = 0.84$), and construct coverage (Jaccard = 0.91) all remain in acceptable ranges. At 20\\%, reliability drops to 0.82---near the psychometric threshold of 0.80. At 50\\%, reliability falls to 0.74, below any acceptable standard.\n\nThe theoretical upper bound under perfect calibration is 25\\%, but imperfect item generation quality (Section~\\ref{sec:item-gen}) compresses this to a practical limit of 15\\%. This constraint covers approximately 80\\% of items with $\\delta \\geq 1.0$, sufficient for most contamination remediation scenarios.\n\n\\subsection{Item-Focused Trees}\n\\label{sec:ift}\n\nAs a methodological robustness check, we compare Item-Focused Trees (continuous covariate) against categorical ETS C DIF (Table~\\ref{tab:ift-comparison}).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Item-Focused Trees vs.\\ categorical DIF.}\n\\label{tab:ift-comparison}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & TPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & TPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.0$) & FPR & Unique Detections \\\\\n\\midrule\nETS C (categorical) & 0.58 & 0.41 & 0.062 & 47 \\\\\nIFT (continuous) & 0.62 & 0.46 & 0.058 & 134 \\\\\nOverlap (both detect) & --- & --- & --- & 612 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nIFT provides a modest improvement (+0.04 TPR, $-$0.004 FPR) by avoiding information loss from arbitrary cohort boundaries. Its primary qualitative contribution is identifying 134 items with \\emph{gradual} contamination---items whose DIF effect sizes increase smoothly over time rather than appearing suddenly. The modal split point (September 2023) approximately corresponds to when MMLU entered mainstream LLM training pipelines.\n\n%===============================================================================\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n%===============================================================================\n\n\\paragraph{Summary of the framework.}\nOur results support a psychometric monitoring framework for benchmark contamination. DIF provides a complementary contamination signal that is uniquely RL-robust among detection methods (retention 0.885), though it is not the sole survivor---ConStat (0.841) and even DVD (0.903, from a low baseline) retain meaningful power. The dual-signal ensemble (DIF + ConStat) achieves TPR = 0.68 across the effect-size spectrum, and within-subject IRT equating enables contamination-aware item replacement up to 15\\% with acceptable validity degradation.\n\n\\paragraph{What DIF cannot detect.}\nDIF requires sufficient contamination effect sizes ($\\delta \\geq 1.0$) and adequate cohort sizes for statistical power. Small effects ($\\delta < 0.6$) are undetectable by our current framework, though these have minimal impact on model rankings. Pattern-level contamination (memorizing solution strategies rather than specific items) also partially evades DIF, as demonstrated by lower power on GSM8K.\n\n\\paragraph{DVD failure: a format dependency.}\nDVD's failure on MMLU is instructive. Generation variance is a valid contamination signal for open-ended benchmarks where memorization produces deterministic long-form output. On multiple-choice formats, the signal is compressed to near-zero by the forced-choice response structure. This highlights that no single contamination detector is format-universal, reinforcing the need for benchmark-specific ensemble design.\n\n\\paragraph{Equating scope limitations.}\nOur equating results are limited to within-subject replacement ($\\rho = 0.93$). Cross-subject equating ($\\rho = 0.87$) is insufficient for full-benchmark score comparability because model evolution changes ability structure, not just ability level---23\\% of MMLU subjects show unstable factor structures across cohorts. Practical deployment should use subject-specific equating rather than full-benchmark equating.\n\n\\paragraph{Generated item quality.}\nReplacement items show systematic quality gaps: lower discrimination ($-0.27$), lower factor loadings ($-0.09$), and weaker distractors ($-0.17$). These gaps constrain the replacement rate to $\\leq$15\\% and indicate that improving item generation quality is the primary bottleneck for expanding the framework's operating envelope.\n\n\\paragraph{RL robustness caveats.}\nOur RL validation uses $n = 35$ models with proxy RL intensity measures. The finding that DIF is RL-robust should be interpreted as ``DIF degrades less than alternatives'' rather than ``DIF is immune to RL.'' Heavy RL ($\\geq$200 steps) reduces DIF TPR from 0.61 to 0.51---a meaningful decline. Controlled RL fine-tuning experiments with known contamination status are needed for definitive validation.\n\n\\paragraph{Temporal monitoring tradeoffs.}\nCumulative Bayesian DIF achieves useful power (0.66--0.78) after four administrations, but this requires 1--2 years of data accumulation. During this period, contamination may have already degraded benchmark validity. The framework is better suited for ongoing monitoring and historical analysis than for rapid real-time detection.\n\n\\paragraph{FPR considerations.}\nThe ensemble FPR of 0.082 approaches the 0.10 threshold for practical concern. On MMLU's 11,847 items, this implies $\\sim$970 false alarms, which could trigger unnecessary item replacement. Threshold tuning and FPR-controlled procedures are important for deployment.\n\n%===============================================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n%===============================================================================\n\nWe have presented a psychometric monitoring framework for LLM benchmark contamination that provides three contributions: (1) empirical evidence that DIF is a complementary contamination signal with the strongest RL-robustness among tested methods (retention ratio 0.885); (2) a dual-signal ensemble (DIF + ConStat) achieving 0.68 full-spectrum TPR with transparent failure mode analysis of DVD on multiple-choice formats; and (3) a within-subject bifactor IRT equating pilot establishing 15\\% item replacement as the practical operating bound ($\\tau \\geq 0.93$, $\\alpha \\geq 0.84$).\n\nThe framework's value lies not in claiming benchmarks can ``self-heal,'' but in providing known operating bounds for contamination-aware recalibration. Key limitations---moderate detection power for medium effects, small RL validation sample, cross-subject equating instability, and generated item quality gaps---define a clear path for future work: larger-scale RL validation, improved item generation, and extension to open-ended benchmarks where DVD may contribute effectively.\n\n%===============================================================================\n% References\n%===============================================================================\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\\begin{thebibliography}{99}\n\n\\bibitem[Brown et al.(2020)]{brown2020gpt3}\nBrown, T., Mann, B., Ryder, N., et al. (2020).\nLanguage models are few-shot learners.\n\\emph{NeurIPS}.\n\n\\bibitem[Dekoninck et al.(2024)]{dekoninck2024constat}\nDekoninck, J., Lunardi, L., Tsitsulin, A., et al. (2024).\nConStat: Performance-based contamination detection in large language models.\n\\emph{NeurIPS}.\n\n\\bibitem[Dodge et al.(2021)]{dodge2021documenting}\nDodge, J., Sap, M., Marasovic, A., et al. (2021).\nDocumenting large webtext corpora: A case study on the Colossal Clean Crawled Corpus.\n\\emph{EMNLP}.\n\n\\bibitem[Dong et al.(2024)]{dong2024dvd}\nDong, Y., et al. (2024).\nGeneralization or memorization: Data contamination and trustworthy evaluation for large language models.\n\\emph{ACL}.\n\n\\bibitem[Gibbons \\& Hedeker(1992)]{gibbons1992bifactor}\nGibbons, R.~D. \\& Hedeker, D.~R. (1992).\nFull-information item bifactor analysis.\n\\emph{Psychometrika}, 57(3), 423--436.\n\n\\bibitem[Holland \\& Thayer(1988)]{holland1988dif}\nHolland, P.~W. \\& Thayer, D.~T. (1988).\nDifferential item performance and the Mantel--Haenszel procedure.\nIn \\emph{Test Validity}. Erlbaum.\n\n\\bibitem[Kolen \\& Brennan(2014)]{kolen2014equating}\nKolen, M.~J. \\& Brennan, R.~L. (2014).\n\\emph{Test Equating, Scaling, and Linking}. Springer.\n\n\\bibitem[Lalor et al.(2019)]{lalor2019irt}\nLalor, J.~P., Wu, H., \\& Yu, H. (2019).\nLearning latent parameters without human response patterns: Item response theory with artificial crowds.\n\\emph{EMNLP}.\n\n\\bibitem[Polo et al.(2026)]{polo2026growing}\nPolo, F.~M., et al. (2026).\nGrowing pains: Understanding the limitations of scalable evaluation.\n\\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n\n\\bibitem[Rodriguez et al.(2021)]{rodriguez2021irt}\nRodriguez, P., Barrow, J., Hoyle, A.~M., et al. (2021).\nEvaluation examples are not equally informative: How should that change NLP leaderboards?\n\\emph{ACL}.\n\n\\bibitem[Shi et al.(2024)]{shi2024mink}\nShi, W., Ajith, A., Xia, M., et al. (2024).\nDetecting pretraining data from large language models.\n\\emph{ICLR}.\n\n\\bibitem[Swaminathan \\& Rogers(1990)]{swaminathan1990dif}\nSwaminathan, H. \\& Rogers, H.~J. (1990).\nDetecting differential item functioning using logistic regression procedures.\n\\emph{Journal of Educational Measurement}, 27(4), 361--370.\n\n\\bibitem[Tutz \\& Berger(2016)]{tutz2016ift}\nTutz, G. \\& Berger, M. (2016).\nItem-focused trees for the identification of items in differential item functioning.\n\\emph{Psychometrika}, 81(3), 727--750.\n\n\\bibitem[Vania et al.(2021)]{vania2021irt}\nVania, C., et al. (2021).\nComparing test sets with item response theory.\n\\emph{ACL}.\n\n\\bibitem[Wang et al.(2026)]{wang2026rl}\nWang, Y., et al. (2026).\nReinforcement learning conceals benchmark contamination.\n\\emph{ICLR}.\n\n\\bibitem[White et al.(2024)]{white2024livebench}\nWhite, C., et al. (2024).\nLiveBench: A challenging, contamination-free LLM benchmark.\n\\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n\n\\bibitem[Zhu et al.(2024)]{zhu2024dyval}\nZhu, K., et al. (2024).\nDyVal: Dynamic evaluation of large language models for reasoning tasks.\n\\emph{ICLR}.\n\n\\bibitem[Zhuang et al.(2023)]{zhuang2023cat}\nZhuang, Y., et al. (2023).\nEfficiently measuring the cognitive ability of LLMs: An adaptive testing perspective.\n\\emph{arXiv preprint}.\n\n\\bibitem[Zieky(1993)]{zieky1993ets}\nZieky, M.~J. (1993).\nPractical questions in the use of DIF statistics in test development.\nIn \\emph{Differential Item Functioning}. Erlbaum.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
